Guided Exercise: Selecting a Storage Class for an Application
Goal of the Lab
Deploy applications with persistent storage using different storage classes.
You will:
•	Deploy MySQL using block storage
•	Deploy web apps using NFS shared storage
•	Share a PVC between applications
________________________________________
Step 1: Prepare the Lab Environment
Run:
lab start storage-classes
This command:
•	Prepares OpenShift cluster
•	Creates project storage-classes
•	Ensures storage classes exist
________________________________________
Step 2: Login to OpenShift
oc login -u developer -p developer \
https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Switch project:
oc project storage-classes
Now all resources will be created inside this project.
________________________________________
Step 3: Check Available Storage Classes
Run:
oc get sc
Example output:
StorageClass	Provisioner
lvms-vg1	topolvm.io
nfs-storage (default)	nfs-subdir-external-provisioner
Meaning:
lvms-vg1
•	Local block storage
•	High performance
•	Good for databases
nfs-storage
•	Shared file storage
•	Good for web servers and shared data
________________________________________
Step 4: Inspect Block Storage Class
Run:
oc describe sc lvms-vg1
Important fields:
Field	Meaning
Provisioner	topolvm.io
ReclaimPolicy	Delete
AllowVolumeExpansion	True
VolumeBindingMode	WaitForFirstConsumer
Important description:
Provides RWO and RWOP Filesystem & Block volumes
Meaning:
This storage supports:
•	Block volumes
•	Filesystem volumes
Block volumes give better performance for databases.
________________________________________
Step 5: Deploy MySQL Database
Create MySQL deployment:
oc create deployment db-pod \
--port 3306 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80
Deployment created.
________________________________________
Step 6: Add Environment Variables
MySQL requires configuration variables.
Run:
oc set env deployment/db-pod \
MYSQL_USER=user1 \
MYSQL_PASSWORD=redhat123 \
MYSQL_DATABASE=items
Meaning:
Variable	Purpose
MYSQL_USER	database username
MYSQL_PASSWORD	user password
MYSQL_DATABASE	database name
________________________________________
Step 7: Verify Pod Running
oc get pods
Example output:
db-pod-b4ccfb74-nn4s5   Running
MySQL container is now active.
________________________________________
Step 8: Create Service for MySQL
Expose deployment:
oc expose deployment/db-pod
This creates a service:
service/db-pod
Service allows network access to the database.
________________________________________
Step 9: Create PVC with Block Storage
Now attach storage to MySQL.
Run:
oc set volumes deployment/db-pod \
--add \
--name lvm-storage \
--type pvc \
--claim-mode rwo \
--claim-size 1Gi \
--mount-path /var/lib/mysql \
--claim-class lvms-vg1 \
--claim-name db-pod-pvc
Explanation:
Option	Meaning
--claim-class lvms-vg1	use block storage
--claim-size 1Gi	request 1GB storage
--mount-path /var/lib/mysql	MySQL data directory
--claim-mode rwo	ReadWriteOnce
Storage flow becomes:
MySQL Pod
   ↓
PVC (db-pod-pvc)
   ↓
StorageClass (lvms-vg1)
   ↓
PV created dynamically
   ↓
Local block storage
________________________________________
Step 10: Check PVC
Run:
oc get pvc
Example:
NAME       STATUS   VOLUME      CAPACITY
db-pod-pvc Bound    pvc-72...   1Gi
Meaning:
•	PVC created
•	PV automatically created
•	Storage bound successfully
________________________________________
Step 11: Inspect PVC
Run:
oc describe pvc db-pod-pvc
Important output:
StorageClass: lvms-vg1
Access Modes: RWO
VolumeMode: Filesystem
Used By: db-pod
Key point:
Used By: db-pod
This confirms MySQL is using this storage.
________________________________________
Step 12: Test Database Connection
Run:
oc run query-db -it --rm \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel8/mysql-80 \
--restart Never --command \
-- /bin/bash -c "mysql -uuser1 -predhat123 \
--protocol tcp -h db-pod -P3306 items \
-e 'show databases;'"
Output:
information_schema
items
performance_schema
Database is working.
________________________________________
Step 13: Delete MySQL Deployment
Delete deployment:
oc delete deployment db-pod
Delete service:
oc delete service db-pod
________________________________________
Step 14: Verify PVC Still Exists
Run:
oc get pvc
Output:
db-pod-pvc Bound
Important concept:
PVC still exists even though pod is deleted.
This proves persistent storage works.
________________________________________
Step 15: Delete PVC
oc delete pvc db-pod-pvc
Because reclaim policy = Delete
Result:
PV deleted automatically
Storage deleted
________________________________________
Step 16: Create Shared Storage PVC (NFS)
Create file:
nfs-pvc.yaml
YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: nfs-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
  storageClassName: nfs-storage
Create PVC:
oc create -f nfs-pvc.yaml
________________________________________
Step 17: Inspect NFS PVC
oc describe pvc nfs-pvc
Important output:
StorageClass: nfs-storage
Status: Bound
Used By: <none>
Meaning:
PVC exists but no pod is using it yet.
________________________________________
Step 18: Deploy Web Server
Create web deployment:
oc create deployment web-pod \
--port 8080 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/httpd-24:1-321
Create service:
oc expose deployment web-pod
Create route:
oc expose svc web-pod \
--hostname web-pod.apps.ocp4.example.com
________________________________________
Step 19: Verify Web Server
Run:
curl http://web-pod.apps.ocp4.example.com
You will see the Apache test page.
________________________________________
Step 20: Mount NFS Storage to Web Server
Run:
oc set volumes deployment/web-pod \
--add \
--name nfs-volume \
--claim-name nfs-pvc \
--mount-path /var/www/html
Meaning:
NFS Storage
     ↓
Mounted to
     ↓
/var/www/html
Apache serves files from this directory.
________________________________________
Step 21: Deploy Second Application
Create deployment:
oc create deployment app-pod \
--port 9090 \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/do180-roster
Create service:
oc expose deployment app-pod
Create route:
oc expose svc app-pod \
--hostname app-pod.apps.ocp4.example.com
________________________________________
Step 22: Mount Same PVC to Second App
Run:
oc set volumes deployment/app-pod \
--add \
--name nfs-volume \
--claim-name nfs-pvc \
--mount-path /var/tmp
Now both applications share storage.
NFS Storage
     ↓
PVC (nfs-pvc)
     ↓
Web Pod → /var/www/html
App Pod → /var/tmp
________________________________________
Step 23: Test Shared Storage
Open browser:
http://app-pod.apps.ocp4.example.com
Enter information and click:
push
This creates:
/var/tmp/People.html
________________________________________
Step 24: Verify Shared Storage
Open:
http://web-pod.apps.ocp4.example.com/People.html
The web server displays the file.
This proves:
Both pods share the same storage
________________________________________
Important Concept Learned
Kubernetes does NOT protect data integrity.
Two apps writing same file can cause corruption.
In this lab:
app-pod → writer
web-pod → reader
So no conflict occurs.
